From 324a699eb0f31dea96140f70f1bfc9c76d7ebfb1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cycles Houdini engine authoring <engine@localhost>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 00:12:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 95/96] Keep object attribute tables when object attributes are
 unchanged

Every geometry update rebuilt the per-object attribute requests and values
for all objects, rewrote their values into the packed tables and uploaded
those tables, even when only object transforms changed. With many instances
carrying object attributes this dominates the attribute stage of a frame
change.

Object attributes are plain values, not sockets, so no update flag tells
whether they changed. Instead, before converting them, check that the
geometry part of every table kept its layout, and that every object would
produce the requests of the last completed update: the same names, types,
motion sample counts and bake hold, in the same order. Their table offsets
are then unchanged, so reuse the previous requests and write the values
directly at those offsets, in parallel. Where a table kept its allocation,
only differing values are written and the table is uploaded only if one
differs. Otherwise convert all objects as before. The attribute map is still
emitted for all objects and object offsets are still written.

Rationale: Rebuilding and re-uploading unchanged object attributes for every object on every transform-only update costs time linear in the object count.
Contract: Attribute requests, packed table contents, attribute map entries and object attribute offsets are identical to a full rebuild; any change in object attribute names, types, sample counts or hold, in shader requests or in the geometry table layout takes the full rebuild.
Tests: tests/houdini_packed_instance_attribute_smoke.py, tests/houdini_sampled_instance_attribute_smoke.py, tests/houdini_packed_cd_smoke.py, tests/houdini_constant_attribute_smoke.py, tests/houdini_named_attribute_smoke.py, tests/houdini_object_properties_smoke.py, tests/houdini_packed_pose_ipr_smoke.py
